         "source": [
            "## Feature Selection: Candidate Columns for Modelling\n",
            "\n",
            "Before fitting KNN / Decision Tree (Section 3.4) and running feature selection (Section 3.6), we reviewed every column in `listings.csv` and classified it as **keep**, **drop**, or **engineer**, with reasoning tied to our research question (distinguishing multi-listing from single-listing hosts) and to specific numbers computed from our own data.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Dropped — target leakage\n",
            "`host_listings_count`, `calculated_host_listings_count`, `calculated_host_listings_count_entire_homes`, `calculated_host_listings_count_private_rooms`, `calculated_host_listings_count_shared_rooms`\n",
            "\n",
            "These columns are used to *define* `host_type` itself (`host_type = 1 if calculated_host_listings_count == 1 else 0`). Including any of them would let the model trivially read off the answer rather than learn a real pattern (mutual information with the target would be at its theoretical maximum, and a Decision Tree would split on it at depth 1 with ~100% accuracy, since it's a perfect proxy for the label).\n",
            "\n",
            "### Dropped — IDs, URLs, metadata, free text, or entirely empty\n",
            "`id`, `listing_url`, `scrape_id`, `picture_url`, `host_id`, `host_url`, `host_profile_id`, `host_profile_url`, `host_picture_url`, `name`, `host_name`, `description`, `host_about`, `host_location`, `source`, `last_scraped`, `calendar_last_scraped`, `has_availability` (1 unique value), `price_quote_*` columns, and every column that is 100% missing (`neighborhood_overview`, `host_since`, `host_response_time`, `host_response_rate`, `host_acceptance_rate`, `host_thumbnail_url`, `host_neighbourhood`, `host_total_listings_count`, `host_verifications`, `neighbourhood`, `neighbourhood_group_cleansed`, `calendar_updated`, `license`, `instant_bookable`).\n",
            "\n",
            "These carry no usable signal: either they're unique identifiers/text requiring out-of-scope NLP work, or they contain zero information (100% missing).\n",
            "\n",
            "### Dropped — near-constant, low information\n",
            "`host_identity_verified`: 97.7% true for multi-listing hosts vs. 99.5% for single-listing hosts — only a 1.8-point gap, both classes >97% true.\n",
            "`host_has_profile_pic`: 98.0% vs. 94.7% (overall 96.5% true across the whole dataset) — still too close to constant to give a model much to split on.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Dropped — redundant with a kept feature\n",
            "`neighbourhood_cleansed` (30-category suburb name) duplicates the geographic information captured more compactly by our engineered `dist_cbd` feature (see below), and one-hot encoding 30 categories would add unnecessary dimensionality (a real cost for KNN's distance calculations).\n",
            "`minimum_minimum_nights`, `maximum_minimum_nights`, `minimum_maximum_nights`, `maximum_maximum_nights`, `minimum_nights_avg_ntm`, `maximum_nights_avg_ntm` — rolling-window variants of `minimum_nights`/`maximum_nights`; we keep the plain columns only.\n",
            "`availability_30`, `availability_60`, `availability_90`, `availability_eoy` — redundant with `availability_365`, which we keep as the single summary measure.\n",
            "`number_of_reviews_ltm`, `number_of_reviews_l30d`, `number_of_reviews_ly` — redundant with `number_of_reviews`; we additionally keep `reviews_per_month` as a rate-based measure of host activity.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Kept — confirmed by checking actual numbers against host_type\n",
            "- **`host_is_superhost`**: 36.1% of multi-listing hosts are superhosts vs. 23.7% of single-listing hosts — professional hosts are meaningfully more likely to hold superhost status.\n",
            "- **`amenities` → engineered `amenity_count`**: parsed the JSON list and counted items. Multi-listing hosts average 37.75 amenities (median 40) vs. 31.60 (median 31) for single-listing hosts — professional hosts list more amenities.\n",
            "- **`latitude`/`longitude` → engineered `dist_cbd`**: Euclidean distance from Melbourne's CBD. Multi-listing hosts sit closer to the CBD (median 0.0397°) than single-listing hosts (median 0.0577°), consistent with professional hosts concentrating in inner-city areas.\n",
            "- **`price`**: kept (reusing Assignment 1's `parse_price_correct`). Median prices are close ($247.50 multi vs. $236.50 single), but means diverge ($308.29 vs. $334.33) — the mean/median gap signals a skew, likely a small number of high-priced single listings (e.g. luxury whole-homes), noted as a limitation rather than smoothed over.\n",
            "- **`review_scores_rating`** (+ `number_of_reviews` kept alongside it): missingness (17.4%, 4,477 rows) is explained entirely by zero reviews existing yet — a perfect 4,477/4,477 match with `number_of_reviews == 0`, confirming this is not missing at random in a concerning way but a mechanical consequence of having no reviews. We impute missing scores with the dataset median rather than drop the rows, since `number_of_reviews` already and separately encodes \"no track record yet\" for the model. Actual scores: single-listing hosts average slightly higher (4.816) than multi-listing hosts (4.680) — opposite to our initial hypothesis that more experienced professional hosts would score higher.\n",
            "- **`estimated_revenue_l365d`**: not leakage (it is derived from `price` and occupancy estimates, not from anything that defines `host_type`), so distinct from the `calculated_host_listings_count` case. Its missingness (25.5%) perfectly overlaps with `price`'s missingness (6,553/6,553 rows), since it is computed from price. Single-listing hosts actually show a higher median ($9,258) than multi-listing hosts ($8,256), consistent with the price finding above rather than our initial \"reinvestment\" hypothesis. Kept pending the Section 3.3 correlation analysis, since it is likely collinear with `price` — if correlation confirms this, we will drop one of the two before modelling.\n",
