            "# Correlation Analysis\n",
            "We use the host-activity example set (Set C), adapted to our research question: *Can multi-listing (\"professional\") hosts be distinguished from single-listing hosts, and what operating differences show up in the data?*  Our target/proxy is `is_multi_listing`, where 1 denotes a listing whose host has more than one calculated listing and 0 denotes a single-listing host.\n",
            "\n",
            "We deliberately exclude `host_listings_count` and `calculated_host_listings_count` from this set. They define the target, so associating either with `is_multi_listing` would be tautological and would not identify an operating difference. The final variables are therefore `is_multi_listing`, `days_since_last_review`, `reviews_per_month`, and `review_scores_rating`. This produces six unique pairs and 24 required results (six pairs × Pearson, Spearman, Mutual Information, and Normalised Mutual Information).\n",
            "\n",
            "Pearson is appropriate for a binary target here: it is the point-biserial correlation. Spearman provides a rank-based comparison robust to skew and outliers. Mutual Information (MI) detects non-linear association but has no direction; Normalised MI (NMI) requires discrete inputs, so continuous variables are quantile-binned for that method only.\n",
            "\n",
            "We set the NMI rule *before* examining the results: up to ten equal-frequency (quantile) bins for each continuous variable. With the complete sample of 21,251 listings, this is roughly 2,125 observations per requested bin, giving useful resolution without sparse marginal bins and reducing the impact of skew. Tied values may reduce the effective bin count; the code reports that count. The binary target remains unbinned. As a robustness check, we also report NMI using up to five bins; we will only rely on conclusions that remain qualitatively unchanged.\n",
            "\n",
            "`last_review`, `reviews_per_month`, and `review_scores_rating` are jointly missing for listings with no reviews. We do not impute a review date, because no review is not equivalent to either a recent or old review. Consequently, the correlation findings apply only to listings with at least one review; this selection and the listing-level (rather than host-level) unit of analysis are limitations to report.\n",
            "\n"
         ]
